    "text": "<p>A spherically symmetric gravitational systom of particles has mass density&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced open=\"{\" close=\"\"><mtable columnalign=\"left\"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>&#961;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext>&#160;for&#160;</mtext><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8804;</mo><mi>R</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#160;for&#160;</mtext><mi>r</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>R</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced></math>&nbsp;where, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#961;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is a constant. A test mass can undergo circular motion under the influence of the gravitational field of particles. Its speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math> as a function of distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#8734;</mo><mo>)</mo></math> from the centre of the system is represented by</p>",
